WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.860
Let's do a psychology experiment.

00:00:02.240 --> 00:00:05.320
Let's show our audience a scene
consisting of eight shots

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.300
and the story it tells is suspicious
but a little ambiguous

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.740
and open to interpretation.

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:13.280
A girl's in her apartment when a guy
walks suspiciously past her window.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:14.840
She goes outside to check it out.

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:16.460
After the video,
we ask our audience

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.140
a couple open-ended questions

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.480
and see what they think about it.

00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:21.100
How did it make them feel,
did they like it,

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.100
did they not like it,
stuff like that.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.240
Now let's show that same video
to a different audience

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.520
but change one thing.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:31.660
Mirror every shot

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.240
so now every movement left-to-right
becomes right-to-left.

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:37.920
The question is, does this
edit change anything?

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.700
Does character direction affect how
the audience perceives the character

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.280
or the entire scene as a whole?

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:44.620
The answer is yes, and a lot
more than you think.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:46.860
But why does it matter which way he went?

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.340
To understand why direction
change is so important,

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.060
we have to talk about the
three axes of movement.

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.460
There are three directions on screen
a character can move.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:57.060
Right and left, up and down
and forward and backward.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.880
The direction a character moves
matters in a scene.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:02.800
Moving towards the camera shows
power, domination and aggression

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.260
and moving away shows weakness.

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.860
Similarly, characters being looked
down on appear weak

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.000
and high up characters are
much more dominant.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.800
"Now my evil power will be unlimited!

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:12.940
"Can you feel me?"

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:14.060
- "I can feel you."

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:14.880
- "Whoo!"

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:16.500
These two explanations make sense.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.540
Of course someone low and
far away from the camera

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:21.340
will be perceived differently from
someone high up and close.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:22.200
[Child screams]

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.760
[Monster starts screaming as well]

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.040
But the final character movement,
lateral movement,

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.440
going from right to left,
is a lot less obvious.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.100
What makes one side of the screen
different from the other?

00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:32.720
Why does movement
right to left matter?

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:35.140
The answer comes from
time and language.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.260
In Western culture, left to right
indicates the progression of time.

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:39.920
Our language reads from left to right.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.940
Books begin from the left and finish
once we get to the right.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:44.720
In video games, players
start on the left side

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.500
and finish the level on the right.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.420
On any line graph, time increases
as we move right.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.240
In our everyday life,
movement towards the right

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:53.720
indicates time, progress and normality,

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.360
and movement towards the left
indicates moving back in time,

00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.960
abnormality and regression.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.360
From this thinking came the theory
amongst film scholars

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.380
that people will interpret
left-to-right movement

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:04.700
as more natural and normal than
right-to-left movement

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.820
because of how our brains naturally
process moving images.

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:10.920
And this idea of time and normality
in left-to-right movement gets applied

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:12.420
in a variety of films.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.460
For example: Lord of the Rings maintains
its left-to-right movement

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:17.860
during the journey from the Shire
to Mount Doom.

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:20.680
Left-right movement is maintained
during the big travel shots

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.200
and it's a subtle visual to
indicate the clear goal

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:25.000
to reach a specific destination.

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:28.100
Compare that clear objective
to the last scene in Full Metal Jacket.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:29.240
It consists of two shots.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:32.220
One shot of soldiers moving
left to right past burning buildings,

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.440
and another shot of them
moving right to left

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.240
with burning buildings still
in the background.

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.080
The abrupt lateral direction change

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:40.500
makes it look as if they
randomly switch directions,

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:43.780
and it gives the implication that
the soldiers are wandering aimlessly.

00:02:43.780 --> 00:02:47.080
Stanley Kubrick uses lateral direction
change to make some commentary

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.280
about the army's unclear objectives

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.780
and perhaps commentary on the
Vietnam war as a whole.

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.340
Lateral character movement
can also tell a story.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.900
In one scene in Full Metal Jacket,
soldiers move to the right

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.020
towards some combat
zone off-screen

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.580
and wounded soldiers get carried
away from the combat zone,

00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.040
moving from the right
side to the left.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:04.880
There's a subtle visual
reinforcement here

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.380
that where the soldiers headed off-screen

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:08.320
is where men get brought back wounded.

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.040
This lateral technique is similarly done subtly
in Platoon and Saving Private Ryan.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:26.940
[music plays, indisctinct voices talking]

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.080
"Wade!"
- "Yes, sir."

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:30.760
- "See what you can do."
- "Yes, sir."

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:33.800
So left to right is observed
as positive, progressive and normal,

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.940
or at least that's what many
film theorists assumed.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.660
It's only in the last five years or so

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:40.460
that researchers started
empirically testing lateral movement.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.660
In 2012, Cleveland State University
conducted an experiment

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:46.220
by showing a sequence of eight
shots to a group of people.

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.540
They showed the exact same shots
to another group of people,

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:51.660
but reversed all left-to-right movement
to right-to-left movement.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.840
Each group answered
several specific questions

00:03:53.840 --> 00:03:56.880
and several open-ended ones
about how they felt after watching.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.440
The results confirmed what
many film theorists had assumed

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.440
but had never had any hard
evidence to prove.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.940
The experimenters analyzed open-ended
questions for key words.

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:09.580
For example, 24.8% of responders used
"anxious," "nervous," "uneasy," "suspenseful"

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:10.500
to describe the video

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:13.600
and 11.9% said "fearful," "frightened" or "scared."

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:16.740
The most statistically significant difference
between the two groups

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:19.160
was what the experimenters
called "the negative effect,"

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:23.500
similar phrases including "didn't like the clip,"
"liked the clip," "good," "bad," "interesting," "boring"

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.680
and "strong" and "weak" were
the phrases that differed

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.160
the most between the two videos.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:30.180
So for the left-to-right video,
members consistently used words

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:31.880
like "good," "interesting" and "strong,"

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.020
while the ones who saw right-to-left,
used words like "bad," "boring" and "weak."

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:39.720
Audiences perceived left-to-right movement
positively and right-to-left movement negatively.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.480
This study provides a huge
opportunity for film makers.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.020
By something as simple as
switching character movement,

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:48.200
a director can instill a negative
or positive emotional response

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:49.220
on the audience.

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:51.440
But it seems some directors have
already caught on this difference

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:52.920
in left to right and right to left,

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:54.980
going as back as Alfred Hitchcock.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:59.020
Alfred Hitchcock's Stranger on a Train opens
with the protagonist and antagonist

00:04:59.020 --> 00:05:01.140
showing only their feet and legs walking.

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.720
They walk towards each other,
as if to imply their paths will eventually cross.

00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.820
If our study is true and left-to-right movement
is preceived as more positive than right-to-left,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:12.100
then this opening scene has
already subconsciously established

00:05:12.100 --> 00:05:14.340
that the right-to-left character
is an antagonist

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.420
and the left-to-right character
is a protagonist.

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:18.880
So not only can left-to-right movement
indicate a progression of time,

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:21.380
it can even indicate something
positive or negative.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:23.880
Roger Ebert fully supported the idea himself,

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.940
as he explains in his 2008 article
"How to Read a Movie."

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:30.040
Roget Ebert goes further to say
even the left and right sides of the screen

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:33.020
are perceived as either more
positive or more negative.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:35.540
A character on the right feels
more positive and in control,

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:38.080
while the one on the left seems
weaker, perhaps more sinister.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:40.980
This is a common recurring theme:
right – good, left – bad,

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.000
and various filmmakers use this technique.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:45.800
"Master's my friend."

00:05:45.800 --> 00:05:48.080
- "You don't have any friends."

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:49.220
"Oh, come off it."

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:50.380
- "You come off it!"

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:51.360
- "You."
- "You."

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:51.940
- "You."

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:52.780
- "You infinity."

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:53.720
- "Ahhh!"

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.320
Ebert makes the claim that
these conventions of right and left

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.500
extend beyond our language and culture.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:06:00.480
He says that in all his years of watching movies,

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.100
Asian cinema uses similar left
versus right techniques.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:04.500
And the study supported this belief,

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.320
religious belief, right or left handedness and race all

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.760
did not significantly affect the
audience's perception of lateral movement.

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:12.800
Of course there are no absolutes in filmmaking

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:15.420
and sometimes what's contrary
to the rule works just as well.

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:17.520
But either way, this study shows that
there really is a difference

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:19.280
between left and right in film.

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:23.600
It's only so recently that any empirical testing
on film techniques has actually been conducted.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.860
Roger Ebert's claims, while convincing,
were only speculation at the time,

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:28.760
but have now been proven to an extent.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:30.440
I contacted Cleveland State University,

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:31.880
the ones who published this study,

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:33.800
and they're still currently conducting experiments,

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.100
testing film theories on audience members.

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:38.560
It's really exciting to see that
even after a hundred years,

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.260
we're still learning new things
about filmmaking to this day.

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:44.540
What was once speculation has
now been followed up with concrete evidence.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:47.140
So the next time a character crosses
the screen, ask yourself:

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:48.420
"Where did he go?"

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:49.840
- "Come on!"
- "Which way did he go?"

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:50.880
- "Where did he go?"
- "Where did he go?"

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.080
- "I don't know, he took my
clothes and went that way."

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:53.940
- "Come on!"

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:57.360
Thanks for watching, I wanted to clarify
the video experiment that I show

00:06:57.360 --> 00:06:58.980
isn't the original video experiment.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:01.620
The original one hasn't been
published yet; it hasn't been posted,

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:04.060
although they published the article,
they haven't posted the video that they used.

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.460
So I just took what they talked about
in the article and I recreated it.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.500
I encourage you guys to look at the original article

00:07:09.500 --> 00:07:12.980
it's amazing, it goes very in depth,
it talks about things I didn't talk about,

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.240
I'll link it in the description,

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:16.760
also I talk about film theorists in the video

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.220
and I don't refer to specific names,

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:19.660
but really I'm referring to Kuleshov.

00:07:19.660 --> 00:07:22.620
He's the man, he's been doing
all this film theory stuff.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:26.600
In the seventies, he was coming up with these–
this three axes of movement theory,

00:07:26.600 --> 00:07:28.700
and how people interepret movement in film

00:07:28.700 --> 00:07:30.140
and so I encourage you to look him up,

00:07:30.140 --> 00:07:32.620
I'm going to add some of his
writing in the description as well.

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:33.760
Thanks guys.

